{"title":"Fermat point","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>Fermat point</b> (also called the Torricelli point) of a triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> (with no angle more than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/4/9b49fcb2f74ccae5ec0e722a8c65b5b9bbd93720.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> is a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> which has the minimum total distance to three <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vertices\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Vertices\">vertices</a> (i.e., <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/f/caf22014818d7d1de241c13e5f1ffc6f5bd86b9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP+BP+CP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"129\" height=\"14\" />).\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/8/ff83e9cf8c39bc33ebf22d8365d8d13a2e7af16c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] pair A=(2,4), B=(1,1), C=(6,1);  pair pAB=rotate(60,B)*A, pCA=rotate(60,A)*C; path PC=pAB--C, PB=pCA--B; D(MP(&quot;A&quot;,A,2N)--MP(&quot;B&quot;,B,SW)--MP(&quot;C&quot;,C,SE)--cycle,green); D(C--pCA--A--pAB--B,red+dashed); DPA(PC^^PB,lightblue); D(MP(&quot;C&#39;&quot;,pAB,NW),orange); D(MP(&quot;B&#39;&quot;,pCA,NE),orange); D(A); D(B); D(C); D(MP(&quot;P&quot;,IP(PC,PB),E),blue); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"168\" />\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Construction\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Construction</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Proofs\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proofs</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Geometric_Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Geometric Proof</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Vector_Proof_(Due_to_Titu_Andreescu_and_Oleg_Mushkarov)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Vector Proof (Due to Titu Andreescu and Oleg Mushkarov)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Generalizations\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Generalizations</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Weighted_Generalization\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Weighted Generalization</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Polygon_Generalization\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Polygon Generalization</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Construction\">Construction</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fermat_point&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Construction\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A method to find the point is to construct three equilateral triangles out of the three sides from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />, then connect each new vertex to each opposite vertex, as these three lines will concur at first Fermat point.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proofs\">Proofs</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fermat_point&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proofs\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We shall present a standard triangle inequality proof as well as a less-known vector proof:\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Geometric_Proof\">Geometric Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fermat_point&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Geometric Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>First, we shall note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> must lie inside the triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />. Otherwise, we suppose that WLOG, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> are on opposite sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />. Then, consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/4/3545551b3d62c4e23d5df7352a55602d0aa9adcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P&#39;$\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> the reflection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />. Note <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/8/318fa6b914a03b894ca8ea19ee804475bf301305.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PB=P&#39;B$\" width=\"86\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/7/c872bbfca171b2011361743763d46565c058af67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PC=P&#39;C$\" width=\"85\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/f/19ffeb38885a307e7510fdb410e69cb34db1a6c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PA&gt;P&#39;A$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"84\" height=\"14\" />, so thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is not the Fermat Point.\n</p><p>Suppose that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b1ac662f7ddbbce489953c8b7693848239564aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"14\" /> was acute. Consider the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/d/08d5c45388de5c75af641c046d466de3592ffa98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> rotation about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b1ac662f7ddbbce489953c8b7693848239564aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"14\" />. For any point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, let the image of this point be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/2/f1263f2cedda790ccaba7be02e2e032f0635f668.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X&#39;$\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. Then, we see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/d/0ad9c6d0f04bff346a0a5c6cdf0b1a59a732d780.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC=AC&#39;$\" width=\"83\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/e/49ed7a3f5fcecb599b5766481d3f0cffd2bd88c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAC&#39;=60^{\\circ}$\" width=\"108\" height=\"14\" />. so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/8/fc8e0a3510428e71f9bbb03c8093a7d5f6c0d500.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACC&#39;$\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> is equilateral. Now, consider the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> inside the triangle. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/4/08463580fa772206f8c1b1b0a6a48a876b392788.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP=AP&#39;$\" width=\"83\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/a/01aa36e35e7a55ab24654601947b95fb94b6b4a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle PAP&#39;=60^{\\circ}$\" width=\"108\" height=\"14\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/8/6588d30823bbdf777e63b500df8f3041d5224336.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP=PP&#39;$\" width=\"84\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, we get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/0/7702ff3e30658f5b2e4b578c603bf20faf80e630.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP+BP+CP=BP+PP&#39;+C&#39;P&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"297\" height=\"15\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/4/2b463005b5c11b5efdf6237fbadba6d7f9520b35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CP=C&#39;P&#39;$\" width=\"89\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Now, WLOG let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/0/9903e66b7f56c0813ada7d46063de50a38a34a77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle B\\leq\\angle C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"79\" height=\"15\" />. We have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/5/2b58b864804dae1f963c958f26fa97e3b7fd7fb2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BCC&#39;\\leq 180^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"118\" height=\"16\" />. We note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/7/0974158403f0954e4a76e431eb8a5c494766c38c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP+BP+CP=BP+PP&#39;+C&#39;P&#39;\\geq BC&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"355\" height=\"16\" /> with equality if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/4/42440a5525d7f080da430c72755522dcaa4f6c07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,P&#39;\\in BC&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"94\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/0/5c0353f0376faba2b99d5ae30faf2535bda6b113.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle APC&#39;=60^{\\circ}$\" width=\"108\" height=\"14\" /> (as then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/0/f80e11f77dc8233a367cbefda0ab5f64b4e89c18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P&#39;\\in CP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"69\" height=\"15\" />). Thus, we see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/1/b31cb0a21f62061c0496f89451c5ea0c14c35ab3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$APCC&#39;$\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is cyclic, so thus as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lies on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/c/5bc856e7f29652b2c928f2c025ed9befbab1873e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC&#39;$\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />, we see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the intersection of the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/8/fc8e0a3510428e71f9bbb03c8093a7d5f6c0d500.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACC&#39;$\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/c/5bc856e7f29652b2c928f2c025ed9befbab1873e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC&#39;$\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> (not <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/7/9b798193cb94562989018728e1151587c618655b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"16\" />). Thus, note that as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/8/ec8b01178d0c64d042cdd732d864ed57c4d82758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle APC&#39;=\\angle CPC&#39;=60^{\\circ}$\" width=\"192\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/2/c82ec9becab2be08113e6d9095f2a77bfc4e77dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle APC=120^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"112\" height=\"13\" />. Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/9/0793a9255424f2f1fe292e76d61bcea7cbc7ac01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CPB=180^{\\circ}-\\angle CPC&#39;=120^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"255\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, we have found the Fermat Point.\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Vector_Proof_.28Due_to_Titu_Andreescu_and_Oleg_Mushkarov.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Vector_Proof_(Due_to_Titu_Andreescu_and_Oleg_Mushkarov)\">Vector Proof (Due to Titu Andreescu and Oleg Mushkarov)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fermat_point&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Vector Proof (Due to Titu Andreescu and Oleg Mushkarov)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We will let our origin be the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/1/9011d933b5a66b4b624cc37b0c6b9f6d710e487a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle APB=\\angle BPC=\\angle CPA=120^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"272\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Consider the point in the plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/5/f6557cd183927406f3dddaefd84716c38e19d318.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=\\vec{A},b=\\vec{B},c=\\vec{C},x=\\vec{X}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"215\" height=\"21\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/d/a2d90b0ab7269c8f0c7109f1c7a3bdfa3a5fa49c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i,j,k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> the unit vectors along <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b29b358e825defa3e11b7d903d43ec31e5909a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/5/4055195bababdc5321118ffd31dad25e1af6e327.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a|=a\\cdot i=(a-x)\\cdot i+x\\cdot i\\leq |a-x|+x\\cdot i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"353\" height=\"18\" />. Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/1/0c1f6f3d7af6ab20fe7eee1cae63f1a4cd6711b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|b|=b\\cdot j=(b-x)\\cdot j+x\\cdot j\\leq |b-x|+x\\cdot j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"354\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/d/2cd8df40a0fbfb7ac94151fbbcb72af4f7949dcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|c|=c\\cdot k=(c-x)\\cdot k+x\\cdot k\\leq |c-x|+x\\cdot k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"361\" height=\"18\" />. Noting that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/3/a33d4ef66d292d0138cdea5708c5df165bc75060.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i+j+k=\\vec{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"104\" height=\"22\" /> and adding, we see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/8/b6892574ff980fcd9d170be1040527a02897027e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a|+|b|+|c|\\leq |a-x|+|b-x|+|c-x|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"320\" height=\"18\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/7/4476537da3f1023b611f5ce3e9faea9bfd5f0527.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP+BP+CP\\leq AX+BX+CX$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"289\" height=\"15\" />. Thus, the origin or point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the desired point.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Generalizations\">Generalizations</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fermat_point&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Generalizations\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are two main generalizations:\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Weighted_Generalization\">Weighted Generalization</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fermat_point&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Weighted Generalization\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The problem goes as following: which point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> minimizes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/3/e234814fada02ebe9f815fb679033dee362e5e9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\cdot AP+n\\cdot BP+p\\cdot CP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"205\" height=\"16\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/6/4864e8028ef28729de83ea8ebdbec75c081986fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n,p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"52\" height=\"11\" /> are positive reals?\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Polygon_Generalization\">Polygon Generalization</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fermat_point&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Polygon Generalization\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The problem goes as following: for the polygon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/a/8dabb3b319a999870941f1f344cb91762000bc28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_2\\cdots A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"91\" height=\"15\" />, which point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> minimizes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/0/400eacc9c6f9e69238efc100a0c4461e3dd38607.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1P+A_2P+\\cdots+A_nP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"195\" height=\"15\" />?\n</p><p>Using the second solution, it is easy to see the point is the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> where the unit vectors to the vertices sum to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. For a quadrilateral, it is the intersection of the diagonals.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fermat_point&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Triangle\" title=\"Triangle\">Triangle</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Point\" title=\"Point\">Point</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Fermat_point&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018054340\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.029 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.042 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 376/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 277/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 12634/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.679      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.679      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4126:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018054340 and revision id 113708. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}